---
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import AskKick from "./AskKick.astro";
import PartHead from "./PartHead.astro";
import NextPart from "./NextPart.astro";
import Beat from "./Beat.astro";
import Touchline from "./Touchline.astro";
import { run, scheduleLog } from "../../lib/first-run";
import { bounds, story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { pipeline, spell, toPlay } from "../../lib/cases";

const sources = [
  ["pages", "Count the matches listed on the pages Kick saved"],
  ["log", "Count the lines in Kick's fetch log"],
  ["rule", "The membership list and the competition's format"],
];
const bar = (n: number) => `${(n / story.scheduled) * 100}%`;
const chain = [
  [story.scheduled, "scheduled by the format", ""],
  [story.finals, "finished", `${toPlay} dated after Friday`],
  [story.finals - bounds.unseen, "on saved hosts' pages", `${bounds.unseen} hosted by Dunmore (Kick read each match only from its host's page)`],
  [story.counted, "counted in Kick's table", "1 finished match with no score on the page (Case 3)"],
] as const;
---

<section id="case-dunmore">
  <PartHead id="case-dunmore">Case 1. Dunmore: a count needs an expected set</PartHead>
  <Pipeline small lit="scrape" note="This case happens at Scrape" />

  <p class="cv-callout">Dunmore athletics: “You've got our men at six conference games. We've played thirteen.” Kick's table had no way to know thirteen was the right number.</p>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Look" title="What the fetch log shows" />
  <p>Besides the table, Kick kept a log of the {run.total} pages it requested. {pipeline.boxes} of those were box scores: Kick's plan also followed the box score links on Alder Ridge's schedule, and no other school's. Here are the {spell(pipeline.asked)} schedule pages:</p>
  <table class="cv-log">
    <caption>Kick's fetch log, schedule pages</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">School</th><th scope="col">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{scheduleLog.map((l) => <tr class:list={{ "is-gap": l.result !== "saved" }}><th scope="row">{l.name}</th><td>{l.result}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The failure sits right there in one line of the log, yet Kick's summary never mentions it. Try asking Kick a follow-up question:</p>
  <AskKick />

  <h4 class="cv-try">How many matches were scheduled?</h4>
  <p>Kick's log names the missing page, but it can't tell you how many matches went missing with it, because nothing in the run records how many matches should exist. To measure the gap in matches, first work out how many the conference scheduled.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="scheduled">
    <p class="cv-kind">Knowledge check</p>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="rule" data-why="The membership list and the competition format. Both exist before the run and independently of it, so a school that never answers stays in the count." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ pages: "A school whose page never answered lists nothing, so it drops out of its own count.", log: "The log only counts pages requested, not matches." })}>
      <label><span>Where can the expected set of matches come from, before the run?</span>
        <select data-save="q-source"><option value="">Choose</option>{sources.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={story.scheduled} data-why={`${story.scheduled}. ${spell(story.schools, true)} hosts, each with ${spell(story.schools - 1)} visitors: ${story.schools} × ${story.schools - 1}. You don't need to fetch a single page to know that.`}>
      <label for="cv-scheduled">Each of the {spell(story.schools)} schools hosts every other school once. How many conference matches are in the full season?</label>
      <input id="cv-scheduled" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="q-scheduled" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer both, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Of those {story.scheduled} scheduled matches, the saved schedules date {spell(toPlay)} for Saturday, November 21, the day after Friday's run.</p></div>
  </div>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Name it" title="The expected set" />
  <p>Kick knew Dunmore's page had failed. What it didn't know was how much went missing with it. Kick had a list of pages, which is why its log could name Dunmore. It had no list of matches, so its {story.counted} matches were compared against nothing. An expected set of matches, taken from the conference format, is the reference that turns that count into coverage.</p>
  <p>Survey statistics has names for the same ideas. The list of units you set out to collect is the <strong>sampling frame</strong>. A unit on that list that never answers is <strong>unit nonresponse</strong>, and Dunmore's schedule page is one.</p>

  <Touchline part="case-dunmore" at={["Fetch"]} note="At Fetch, before the first request." title="Where Touchline gets its expected counts">
    <p>Touchline's expected set of pages comes from a season membership file kept apart from any scraper run, and its expected match totals come from the conference format. Counting whatever appears on saved pages is a weaker fallback, because it is only as complete as the pages that answered.</p>
  </Touchline>

  <Beat part="case-dunmore" name="Your claim" title="Caption Friday's standings" />
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="claim-count">
    <p class="cv-kind">Apply it</p>
    <div data-q data-type="range" data-answer={`${story.counted},${story.finals}`} data-why={`${story.counted} of ${story.finals}. Kick's summary reported ${story.counted}. The denominator is what should exist by now: ${story.scheduled} scheduled, minus ${spell(toPlay)} still to play. Not ${story.scheduled} (unplayed matches aren't missing) and not ${story.counted} (a count of what was saved can't show what wasn't).`}>
      <p class="cv-qlabel">Write the caption Kick's table needed. Kick's summary only stated how many matches it found.</p>
      <p class="cv-row cv-fillin">
        <span>Built from</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Matches in the table" data-save="claim-count" />
        <span>of</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Finished conference matches" data-save="claim-total" />
        <span>finished conference matches.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Enter both numbers to check and see the full breakdown.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check caption</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <div class="cv-chain" role="img" aria-label={chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => `${n} ${what}${lost ? `, minus ${lost}` : ""}`).join(". ")}>
        {chain.map(([n, what, lost]) => <div>{lost && <p class="cv-drop">minus {lost}</p>}<span class="cv-track"><span style={`width:${bar(n)}`}></span></span><p><b>{n}</b> {what}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <p>Riley could have seen every line of this before publishing. With an expected set, any reader can tell the table is short.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <NextPart from="case-dunmore">Dunmore was the only site that failed. So why was Hartwell's row wrong?</NextPart>
</section>
